olunteer ambulance members use blue lights?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"The current text of § 14-96q(d) authorizes a flashing green or blue light for an active member of a volunteer ambulance association or company holding a permit from the chief executive officer of that association. Older guides that say green only reflect a prior version of the statute.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Are flashing white headlights legal in Connecticut?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"A vehicle operated by a member of a volunteer fire department or company, or a volunteer EMT, may use flashing white head lamps when on the way to the scene of a fire or medical emergency, subject to written authorization.\"\n      \t}\n    \t},\n    \t{\n      \t\"@type\": \"Question\",\n      \t\"name\": \"Are tow trucks emergency vehicles in Connecticut?\",\n      \t\"acceptedAnswer\": {\n        \t\"@type\": \"Answer\",\n        \t\"text\": \"No. Tow and wrecker vehicles are not classified as emergency vehicles under Connecticut law. They may display yellow or amber warning lights while working, which provides visibility but no right of way.\"\n      \t}\n    \t}\n  \t]\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"Organization\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#organization\",\n  \t\"name\": \"Strobes N' More\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\",\n  \t\"telephone\": \"+1-401-454-4487\",\n  \t\"email\": \"sales@strobesnmore.com\",\n  \t\"address\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"PostalAddress\",\n    \t\"streetAddress\": \"81 Tom Harvey Road\",\n    \t\"addressLocality\": \"Westerly\",\n    \t\"addressRegion\": \"RI\",\n    \t\"postalCode\": \"02891\",\n    \t\"addressCountry\": \"US\"\n  \t}\n\t},\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"WebSite\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/#website\",\n  \t\"url\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/\",\